

⭐ 1. FULL API SPEC (Developer‑Ready)

This is the formal interface contract for every module.

1.1 Core Simulation API

run_simulation(config, limits, duration, dt, private_key)

Runs a full simulation and returns a sealed record.

Inputs:

SimulationConfig

ConstraintLimits

duration (float)

dt (float)

private_key (bytes)

Returns:

SimulationRecord (dict)

initialize_state(config)

Creates a fresh SystemState object.

serialize_state(state)

Converts internal state to JSON‑safe dict.

1.2 Physics Subsystem APIs

Thermal

step_thermal(state, config, dt)

Structural

step_structural(state, config, dt)

Power

step_power(state, config, dt)

Plasma

step_plasma(state, config, dt)

Environment

step_environment(state, config, dt)

All follow the same signature:

def step_xxx(state: SystemState, config: SimulationConfig, dt: float) -> None

1.3 Constraint API

evaluate_constraints(state, limits)

Returns:

violations (dict)

margins (dict)

1.4 Provenance API

seal_run(config, snapshots, private_key)

Produces:

metadata

hash

signature

snapshots

verify_run(record, public_key)

Returns:

True or False

⭐ 2. DEVELOPER ONBOARDING GUIDE

A clean, 10‑minute ramp‑up for new contributors.

Step 1 — Install dependencies

Python 3.10+NumPyMatplotlib (optional GUI)Cryptography library (for signing)

Step 2 — Understand the architecture

Physics modules update the state

Constraint manager checks limits

Integrator loops over time

Provenance layer seals results

CLI/GUI exposes everything

Step 3 — Run a sample simulation

python cli/main.py simulate --config scenario.json

Step 4 — Validate a run

python cli/main.py validate-run --input sealed_record.json

Step 5 — Extend physics

Add new modules under physics/.

